     "blockId": "p11-identify-block",
     "replacement": "An equation is **homogeneous** in $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ if every term has the same total degree, where the degree of a term is the sum of the powers of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ in it.\nA constant can first be rewritten using $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$. In the third example, $2=2(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)^2$, so all terms can be written with total degree 4.\n\n✗ $\\sin^2x+\\sin x=0$\t✓ $\\sin^2x+\\sin x\\cos x=0$\t✓ $3\\sin^4x-4\\cos^4x=2$\t✗ $\\sin^4x+\\sin^2x\\cos x=2$",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p11-identify-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "af30888ae6df20f74ba8a79be2e4fda1f2e04e4af3edcfa00a27395cb357b5f4"
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-3-further-trigonometric-equations-v1/20261111-124656",
   "revision": 15
  },
  "34716f37-fcfd-43b6-b3ba-0cad82167f2e": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p11-strategies-block",
     "replacement": "To solve, divide every term by $\\cos^nx$, where $n$ is the total degree, to get a polynomial in $\\tan x$.\n\nDividing by $\\cos x$ assumes $\\cos x\\ne0$.\nTherefore, for $x\\in[0,2\\pi)$, **always** check by substitution whether $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ or $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ is a solution.\n\nRemember that $\\sin 2x$ and $\\cos 2x$ expand to give terms of degree 2.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p11-strategies-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "5d3e6868c0c50666403ea16e57c699951f92cb92b2902ba0f9890a868a945ff2",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-3-further-trigonometric-equations-v1/20261111-124656",
   "revision": 15
  },
  "9e668092-0072-480a-91bf-2bee5a2f4710": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p13-squaring-theory",
     "replacement": "Squaring both sides lets you apply a double-angle or Pythagorean identity to simplify the equation.\nThere is _usually_ a better method.\n\nSquaring can introduce extra solutions, so always check your answers by substituting them into the original equation.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p13-squaring-theory",
     "targetFingerprint": "f37530d25858f46fbf0387066e5af40c3c22e82b2422fefd15e8b7dfe232352c",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-3-further-trigonometric-equations-v1/20261111-124656",
   "revision": 15
  },
  "fd9d182a-38b0-4ec9-b2ed-db6d4e6258b5": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p23-theory-block",
     "replacement": "Given $R\\cos\\alpha=x,\\ R\\sin\\alpha=y$ with $x\\ne0$, we solve for $\\alpha$ by dividing the sine equation by the cosine equation.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\sin\\alpha}{R\\cos\\alpha}&=\\frac{y}{x}\\\\\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis eliminates $R$ and leaves an equation with only one trigonometric function: $\\tan\\alpha$.\n\nTo find the quadrant, use the signs of $\\cos\\alpha$ and $\\sin\\alpha$ (the signs of $x$ and $y$, since $R>0$). If $x=0$, then $\\alpha=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ when $y>0$ and $\\alpha=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ when $y<0$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p23-theory-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "26235094a043d4601a97e1de90247131bc0b4ca7889dc128a3560dee2a9b3e45",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-3-further-trigonometric-equations-v1/20261111-124656",
   "revision": 15
  },
  "c86f46c9-d966-4ed9-8531-5b91ebfcf4f6": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p24-method",
     "replacement": "1. Expand $R\\sin(x\\pm\\alpha)$ or $R\\cos(x\\pm\\alpha)$ using the compound angle formula.\n2. Equate coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ to form equations for $R$ and $\\alpha$.\n3. Solve for $R$ and $\\alpha$ simultaneously.\n  To find $R$: Square and add the equations, then use the Pythagorean identity. Take $R>0$.\n  To find $\\alpha$: Divide the two equations to make a single equation in $\\tan\\alpha$.\n  Identify the quadrant using the signs in step 2.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p24-method",
     "targetFingerprint": "a257d685bffbb492e94ba09475328f9e2f3bb232d471c5dca2ba18a188795116",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-3-further-trigonometric-equations-v1/20261111-124656",
   "revision": 15
  },
  "0270d467-5265-4d39-9e8f-a8cd4953fa8e": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p24-worked-example",
     "nodeId": "p24-example-body",
     "replacement": "1. Expand:\n\n$$R\\sin(x+\\alpha)=R\\sin x\\cos\\alpha+R\\cos x\\sin\\alpha$$\n\n2. Equate coefficients:\n\n$$\\sin x-\\sqrt{3}\\cos x=R\\sin x\\cos\\alpha+R\\cos x\\sin\\alpha$$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}R\\cos\\alpha&=1\\\\R\\sin\\alpha&=-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}$$\n\n3. Find $R$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}R^2\\cos^2\\alpha+R^2\\sin^2\\alpha&=1^2+(-\\sqrt{3})^2\\\\R^2(\\cos^2\\alpha+\\sin^2\\alpha)&=4\\\\R^2&=4\\\\R&=2\\quad(R>0)\\end{aligned}$$\nFind $\\alpha$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\sin\\alpha}{R\\cos\\alpha}&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}\\\\\\tan\\alpha&=-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nReference angle: $\\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3})=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$\n\n$$\\sin\\alpha<0,\\quad\\cos\\alpha>0,\\quad\\therefore\\mathrm{Q4}$$\n\n$$\\alpha=2\\pi-\\frac{\\pi}{3}=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$$\n\n$$\\therefore\\sin x-\\sqrt{3}\\cos x=2\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)$$",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p24-worked-example",
     "targetFingerprint": "a966abafc75f818b9351e68002337bbc5d2e34186b0c67658de1575eaa8102d3"
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-3-further-trigonometric-equations-v1/20261111-124656",
